WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
"Does Sugar Lead to Weight Gain?"

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:13.839
The obesity epidemic may 
just be the tip of the iceberg

00:00:13.839 --> 00:00:16.090
in terms of excess body fat.

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:20.200
It's been estimated that 91%
of adults—9 out of 10 of us—

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:25.809
and 69% of children in the
United States are "overfat,"

00:00:25.809 --> 00:00:30.759
defined as excess body fat sufficient
to impair health that can occur

00:00:30.759 --> 00:00:36.340
even in normal weight individuals,
often due to excess abdominal fat.

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:38.080
The way to tell if you're overfat

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:43.490
is if your waist circumference
is more than half your height.

00:00:43.490 --> 00:00:46.090
What's causing this epidemic?

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:49.730
One primary cause may be all
the added sugars we're eating.

00:00:49.730 --> 00:00:51.820
A century ago, sugar was heralded

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:55.000
as one of the cheapest
forms of calories in the diet.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:59.579
Just ten cents' worth of sugar could
furnish thousands of calories.

00:00:59.579 --> 00:01:04.780
Harvard's sugar-pushing nutritionist
bristled at the term empty calories.

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:08.460
The calories in sugar were
"not empty but full of energy."

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:13.600
In other words, full of calories, which
we now are getting too many of.

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:16.780
The excess body weight of the
US population corresponds

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:23.429
to about a 350 - 500 calorie excess
daily caloric intake on average.

00:01:23.429 --> 00:01:26.200
So, to revert the obesity epidemic,

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:28.429
that's how many calories
we have to reduce.

00:01:28.429 --> 00:01:32.700
OK, so which calories
should we cut?

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:35.609
That's just how many calories the
majority of Americans who fail to meet

00:01:35.609 --> 00:01:39.240
the Dietary Guidelines sugar limit
get in added sugars every day.

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:42.689
25 teaspoons is about 400 calories.

00:01:42.689 --> 00:01:45.770
Even the most die-hard sugar
defenders, like James Rippe,

00:01:45.770 --> 00:01:51.749
who was reportedly paid $40,000 a month
by the high fructose corn syrup industry,

00:01:51.749 --> 00:01:55.490
on top of the $10 million
they paid for his research,

00:01:55.490 --> 00:02:01.299
even Dr. Rippe considers it indisputable
that sugars contribute to obesity.

00:02:01.299 --> 00:02:03.990
It is also indisputable
that sugar reduction

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:07.509
should be part of any
weight loss program.

00:02:07.509 --> 00:02:09.810
And of all sources
of calories to limit—

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:14.459
since sugar is just empty calories,
contains no essential nutrients—

00:02:14.459 --> 00:02:18.400
reducing sugar consumption
is obviously the place to start.

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:20.489
And again, this is what
the researchers funded

00:02:20.489 --> 00:02:25.310
by the likes of Dr. Pepper
and Coca-Cola are saying.

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:29.290
The primary author, Richard Kahn,
is infamous for his defense

00:02:29.290 --> 00:02:32.290
of the American Beverage
Association, the soda industry.

00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:36.200
He was chief science officer at
the American Diabetes Association

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:38.540
when they signed a million-dollar
sponsorship deal

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:41.519
with the world's largest
candy company.

00:02:41.519 --> 00:02:44.430
Maybe the American Diabetes
Association should rename itself

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:47.829
the American Junk Food Association.

00:02:47.829 --> 00:02:49.189
But what do you expect
from an organization

00:02:49.189 --> 00:02:52.360
that was started with
drug industry funding?

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:55.370
The bottom line is that randomized,
controlled trials show that increasing

00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:59.480
sugars intake increases calorie intake,
and this leads to body weight gain

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:03.989
in adults, and sugar reduction leads
to body weight loss in children.

00:03:03.989 --> 00:03:06.939
For example, when researchers
randomize individuals to either

00:03:06.939 --> 00:03:10.189
increase their intake of table
sugar or decrease their intake,

00:03:10.189 --> 00:03:14.470
the added sugar group gained about
3.5 pounds over 10 weeks,

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:17.890
whereas the reduced sugar 
group lost about 2.5 pounds.

00:03:17.890 --> 00:03:22.599
A systematic review and meta-analysis
of all such "ad libitum diet" studies

00:03:22.599 --> 00:03:25.640
(meaning real-life studies where
sugar levels were changed

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:29.239
but people could otherwise
eat whatever they wanted)

00:03:29.239 --> 00:03:33.670
reduced intake of dietary sugars resulted
in a decrease in body weight, whereas

00:03:33.670 --> 00:03:37.849
increased sugars intake resulted
in a comparable increase in weight.

00:03:37.849 --> 00:03:41.100
The researchers conclude that 
considering the rapid weight gain

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:43.560
that occurs after an 
increased intake of sugars,

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:48.040
it seems reasonable to
advise people to cut down.

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:52.359
Findings from observational studies
have been more ambiguous though

00:03:52.359 --> 00:03:57.060
with an association found between
obesity and sweetened beverage intake,

00:03:57.060 --> 00:04:01.690
but failing to show consistent
correlations with sugary foods.

00:04:01.690 --> 00:04:04.659
Most such studies rely on
self-reported data, however,

00:04:04.659 --> 00:04:08.879
and obese people tend to
under-report sugar rich foods.

00:04:08.879 --> 00:04:12.879
One can measure trace sucrose
levels in the urine, however,

00:04:12.879 --> 00:04:17.000
to not only get an objective measure
of actual sugar intake, but to exclude

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:22.140
contributions from other sweeteners
such as high fructose corn syrup.

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:25.990
When researchers have done this, they
discovered that sugar intake is indeed

00:04:25.990 --> 00:04:29.530
not only associated with greater odds of
obesity and greater waist circumference

00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:32.840
on a snapshot-in-time
cross-sectional basis,

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:37.260
but in a prospective 
cohort study over time.

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:40.190
Using urinary sucrose as
a measure of sucrose intake,

00:04:40.190 --> 00:04:42.730
those in the highest versus the
lowest fifth for sucrose intake

00:04:42.730 --> 00:04:47.860
had more than a 50% greater
risk of being overweight or obese.

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:50.800
Denying evidence that sugars are 
harmful to health has evidently

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:53.400
been at the heart of the 
sugar industry's defense.

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:57.530
But when the evidence is undeniable,
like the link between sugar and cavities,

00:04:57.530 --> 00:04:59.920
they switch from
denial to deflection,

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:03.110
like trying to switch attention
from restricting intake

00:05:03.110 --> 00:05:07.250
to coming up with some kind
of vaccine against tooth decay.

00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:09.970
We seem to have reached
a similar point with obesity,

00:05:09.970 --> 00:05:13.690
with the likes of the Sugar Bureau
switching from denial to deflection

00:05:13.690 --> 00:05:16.670
by commissioning research
suggesting obese individuals

00:05:16.670 --> 00:05:19.260
would not benefit
from losing weight,

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:21.950
a stance contradicted
by hundreds of studies

00:05:21.950 --> 00:05:27.000
across four continents involving
more than ten million participants.

